1
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:20,470
just say briefly that I'll turn it to you, Peter, on this last one. I don't agree with that last

2
00:01:20,470 --> 00:01:26,670
mode of thinking. I mean, General Motors is not more powerful than South Dakota.

3
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:33,630
South Dakota will be here long after General Motors is gone. And in any conflict between

4
00:01:33,630 --> 00:01:40,560
South Dakota and General Motors, South Dakota is more likely to win. What is the power of General

5
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:46,480
Motors? I mean, what is the power of Chrysler? I mean, we could have had a discussion about Ford

6
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:54,640
two years ago, what does the power afford? There's no power there in the political sense.

7
00:01:55,560 --> 00:02:06,910
It has the large corporation, despite what Ken Galbraith says, is to a very large degree a

8
00:02:06,910 --> 00:02:14,560
prisoner of the market. It's not free to do all that many things. It doesn't have that much power.

9
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:18,240
There is a difference between economic behavior and political behavior.

10
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:25,340
Political behavior, you are, or at least supposed to be, within the realm of freedom to do things that you want to do.

11
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:32,580
And economic behavior, to some degree, or to a very large degree, you don't have that freedom.

12
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:36,280
You have to cope with what the market dictates.

13
00:02:37,130 --> 00:02:44,150
So I don't like the notion of politicizing the large corporation by saying, in effect, you're a state within a state.

14
00:02:44,950 --> 00:02:47,570
you're a mini-government,

15
00:02:47,890 --> 00:02:50,630
and you should start thinking like a mini-government

16
00:02:50,630 --> 00:02:52,650
because if they start thinking like a mini-government,

17
00:02:52,690 --> 00:02:53,790
then they'll become a branch of government.

18
00:02:54,270 --> 00:02:55,830
And we have enough branches of government.

19
00:03:03,930 --> 00:03:07,840
I think that Irving is right and he is wrong.

20
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:15,790
He is absolutely right in saying that nothing is more dangerous

21
00:03:15,790 --> 00:03:28,610
than for any institution to confuse authority and responsibility.

22
00:03:29,170 --> 00:03:39,180
And that, let me see, the one who wants to, not doesn't want to,

23
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:42,140
but if he succeeds, which I don't think he will,

24
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:47,220
would make the corporation our master happens to be Ralph Nader.

25
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:50,880
Ralph Nader is not an enemy of big business.

26
00:03:50,880 --> 00:04:08,530
By claiming that the corporation should be responsible for everything, he wants to give it ultimate authority, since one cannot have responsibility without authority, and I am not willing to give General Motors or anybody else a great deal of authority.

27
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:16,980
And the right thing is to say that institutions should stick to their authority

28
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:20,740
and not claim responsibility beyond it.

29
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:31,580
And the job of a business is to make goods and services at a price the customer is willing to pay

30
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:38,650
and to form the capital the economy needs for tomorrow, for the jobs of tomorrow.

31
00:04:38,650 --> 00:04:44,540
and that's enough and anything beyond that, it is your patient right.

32
00:04:45,890 --> 00:04:51,530
And that is even more conservative in the old sense than Irving is.

33
00:04:51,890 --> 00:04:58,510
And at the same time, in a pluralist society, and ours in this century has become one,

34
00:04:58,970 --> 00:05:03,750
no matter what existing political theory still says or sees,

35
00:05:03,750 --> 00:05:09,510
and it still sees a liberal state without institutions of 1850,

36
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:24,710
In a pluralist society, no society has ever survived unless the individuals in leadership positions took leadership responsibility.

37
00:05:25,530 --> 00:05:27,510
And so you have a built-in problem.

38
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:49,400
On the one hand, if GM or the Citibank or anybody else starts to act like a power, they will very rapidly be disabused of this conceit. Very rapidly. They have no power. They are prisoners of the market on the one hand.

39
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:58,280
And the most minute political power can discipline them very easily.

40
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:04,860
And on the other hand, if the people who are at the head of these institutions,

41
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:11,390
who are the only leadership groups we have in society, are heads of institutions.

42
00:06:11,390 --> 00:06:16,390
This, by the way, is why I don't believe in the power of the intellectuals.

43
00:06:16,390 --> 00:06:22,410
They make a lot of noise, but they have no power base. No.

44
00:06:22,410 --> 00:06:30,130
And they make an enormous amount of noise, no one more than I do, but that's still not the power base.

45
00:06:30,130 --> 00:06:41,840
The confusion between noise and power is endemic in this society, but believe me, a media event is still only a media event.

46
00:06:41,840 --> 00:07:02,400
And so if the leadership people, leadership groups, do not say we are visible, we matter, we have to give witness in the old term, I think that the society cannot survive.

47
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:07,970
It has a built-in conflict, and no pluralist society has ever resolved it.

48
00:07:08,670 --> 00:07:11,130
But if the leadership people do not,

49
00:07:13,370 --> 00:07:16,750
noblesse oblige did not mean to be generous.

50
00:07:17,010 --> 00:07:21,810
It meant to give leadership precisely because you were visible.

51
00:07:22,470 --> 00:07:25,730
And if they don't do it, what other leadership groups are there

52
00:07:25,730 --> 00:07:28,150
except the leaders of our institutions?

53
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:31,540
This is a society of organizations

54
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:38,920
When 90% of the educated people, and that figure comes out of Irving Kristol's magazine,

55
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:41,300
the public interest, so it must be right.

56
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:46,760
When 90% work for institutions, not for corporations,

57
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:52,820
then the leaders of the institutions are the only leadership groups we have.

58
00:07:53,560 --> 00:08:02,650
And if they do not see themselves as responsible for that position and do not live up to it,

59
00:08:02,650 --> 00:08:05,690
then neither they nor the society can survive.

60
00:08:06,170 --> 00:08:10,750
Yes, General Motors better stick to making rust-proof cars,

61
00:08:11,370 --> 00:08:12,850
which they don't do at present.

62
00:08:13,890 --> 00:08:20,650
But the people at the head cannot do what Alfred Sloan still tried to do,

63
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:25,780
who said that I have no authority,

64
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:30,000
and therefore I cannot possibly take responsibility.

65
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:32,240
I'm quoting him, he said that to me.

66
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:39,180
That was in 1944, and I think he had a strong moral position.

67
00:08:40,630 --> 00:08:47,030
I think today, with no other leadership group around,

68
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:57,760
you better accept the fact that, like any other leadership group before,

69
00:08:59,090 --> 00:09:04,950
our business leadership cannot get out of what is an existential tragedy, basically,

70
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:14,310
an unresolvable conflict between authority, which it doesn't have, and responsibility, which it must accept.

71
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:20,220
That's a very moralist point of view, in case you haven't noticed.

72
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:23,240
It's a nice formulation.

73
00:10:09,590 --> 00:10:13,910
Boze Irving, Crystal, and I ended up teaching at business schools

74
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:20,530
because Boze Irving and I, I think, sneakily suspected they are the only real schools of theology around.

75
00:10:22,450 --> 00:10:55,400
I think, for the moment, this is one of the last gasps of the 1960s.

76
00:10:56,060 --> 00:11:02,540
It may change later in the 1980s, but at the moment, the economic problem is the big problem.

77
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:06,500
And those are the issues of, quote, an age of affluence, unquote.

78
00:11:07,020 --> 00:11:11,780
But that age of affluence seems to have vanished somehow before we all got a chance to enjoy it.

79
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:16,860
And in an age of scarcity or an age of difficulty,

80
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:21,100
those are not the issues that are going to concern Congress or the public.

81
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:26,120
And it's interesting, you know, the New York Times wrote up Ralph Nader's day in Washington

82
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:27,140
in the business section.

83
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:29,880
This was extraordinary.

84
00:11:30,940 --> 00:11:35,960
I think this is probably the time to make a public issue of Ralph Nader's stock,

85
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:42,340
but which one always does, you know, when a company has reached the peak earnings.

86
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:47,960
And, no, seriously, I think we can anticipate that in economics,

87
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:54,960
economics are concerned with productivity and supply, and in politics are concerned

88
00:11:54,960 --> 00:12:02,640
with national survival in a singularly dangerous and nasty international world. And then those

89
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:17,680
concerns may get a great deal of publicity, but I don't think that they're going to get

90
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:18,880
much more than that.

91
00:12:37,580 --> 00:13:10,630
It is...

92
00:13:10,630 --> 00:13:11,870
We better repeat the question.

93
00:13:12,050 --> 00:13:12,250
Yeah.

94
00:13:12,870 --> 00:13:18,010
Well, if I understand the question of the last part of the question,

95
00:13:18,090 --> 00:13:20,770
anyhow, it was the fact that it does seem to be a fact

96
00:13:20,770 --> 00:13:23,930
that the governmental bureaucracy of this country

97
00:13:23,930 --> 00:13:26,990
is much more adversarial in its attitude toward business

98
00:13:26,990 --> 00:13:29,210
than, say, not only in Japan, but in Western Europe.

99
00:13:30,230 --> 00:13:33,370
And is this fact...

100
00:13:33,370 --> 00:13:58,890
what is the question about it you take that yes uh the question is why do our bureaucracy

101
00:13:59,450 --> 00:14:04,970
strictly adversary in its view of business and the rest of the world it isn't now this

102
00:14:04,970 --> 00:14:12,330
has long historical tablets but believe me fundamentally being a continental economy

103
00:14:12,330 --> 00:14:21,050
the only one uh we for 200 years managed our affairs on the assumption that there is no life

104
00:14:21,050 --> 00:14:28,990
on this planet outside the United States, which other countries were never fortunate

105
00:14:28,990 --> 00:14:30,050
to indulge in.

106
00:14:30,690 --> 00:14:40,150
And we very rapidly get to the point where we realize that we are not immune, not isolated.

107
00:14:40,890 --> 00:14:44,790
Though, believe me, in this country nobody yet quite sees this.

108
00:14:44,790 --> 00:14:51,630
When you listen to people discussing health care, you are given purely American explanations

109
00:14:51,630 --> 00:15:09,690
Nobody seems to know that countries in which a payments mechanism is exactly different from ours and exactly what is being advocated, let's say, by Senator Kennedy, have the same explosion of health care costs, though the American explanations can't be true.

110
00:15:09,690 --> 00:15:24,210
Nobody seems to realize that the countries that had no Vietnam War and no racial problems had earlier student unrest and more student unrest than we had sorted in France and in Japan.

111
00:15:25,560 --> 00:15:30,940
So we are still thinking in terms of an isolated U.S.

112
00:15:30,940 --> 00:16:01,250
But it's changing very rapidly, and you will very rapidly see perhaps an excessive reaction to the realization that we are very much in a world that is very dangerous, and that therefore the fundamental basic attitudes, which are already changing.

113
00:16:01,890 --> 00:16:07,250
Ten years ago, our response to Chrysler would have been a very different one, for better or worse.

114
00:16:07,930 --> 00:16:14,430
So I think if you want to know what American politics will look like ten years hence,

115
00:16:14,430 --> 00:16:24,010
to a large extent we will have become far more like a European country and far less of an exception,

116
00:16:24,710 --> 00:16:32,040
simply because our politics since Jefferson were based on the assumption

117
00:16:32,040 --> 00:16:43,680
that somehow the outside world is a non-event as far as American politics is concerned.

118
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:45,840
And that's no longer tenable.

119
00:16:47,370 --> 00:16:52,440
The age of both internationalism and isolationism,

120
00:16:52,980 --> 00:16:58,260
both of which assume that we are alone in this world, that age is over.

121
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:39,220
I don't know the five factors.

122
00:17:39,320 --> 00:17:41,540
I can never remember the five causes of the Civil War.

123
00:17:41,540 --> 00:17:44,520
I'd always go blank

124
00:17:44,520 --> 00:17:45,660
when that question appeared

125
00:17:45,660 --> 00:17:47,240
what was the second part

126
00:17:47,240 --> 00:18:06,750
none of the above

127
00:18:06,750 --> 00:18:14,240
no I think that

128
00:18:14,240 --> 00:18:18,140
in the first place

129
00:18:18,140 --> 00:18:20,140
perhaps

130
00:18:20,140 --> 00:18:25,360
it's a good idea to look at your company

131
00:18:25,360 --> 00:18:26,840
and not at the company

132
00:18:26,840 --> 00:18:28,540
to answer these questions

133
00:18:28,540 --> 00:18:30,900
what are the most important things

134
00:18:30,900 --> 00:18:32,420
for your company

135
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:34,880
because I don't think the general answers

136
00:18:34,880 --> 00:18:36,100
will get you to action

137
00:18:36,100 --> 00:18:59,360
And secondly, perhaps, yes, basic assumptions we have all been making about the economy, both domestic and international, about the society, about the political system, are open to serious re-examination.

138
00:18:59,360 --> 00:19:02,900
We'll do some of it tomorrow in the economy.

139
00:19:03,620 --> 00:19:10,340
But I think answers could be very glib,

140
00:19:11,060 --> 00:19:15,520
and if you were to live by them, you'd come across very fast.

141
00:19:16,120 --> 00:19:18,560
Do you agree?

142
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:19,320
I agree.

143
00:19:19,700 --> 00:19:23,680
I think this is a situation in transition

144
00:19:23,680 --> 00:19:30,640
when there's not try to come out with that kind of conclusion.

145
00:19:31,160 --> 00:19:32,980
That leaves it to the historian.

146
00:19:32,980 --> 00:19:35,400
One does it after the event.

147
00:19:35,660 --> 00:20:10,530
at 18th, 19th

148
00:20:10,530 --> 00:20:12,100
magazines.

149
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:15,300
But you're getting down to reading again.

150
00:20:15,500 --> 00:20:17,340
And, of course, that's my favorite way of

151
00:20:17,340 --> 00:20:19,300
being in touch with the outside

152
00:20:19,300 --> 00:20:21,240
world. I think it's even better

153
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:21,860
than television.

154
00:20:23,340 --> 00:20:24,680
But it doesn't beat teaching.

155
00:20:25,380 --> 00:20:27,040
I'm sorry? It doesn't beat teaching.

156
00:20:27,260 --> 00:20:29,220
I think one learns most by teaching.

157
00:20:29,680 --> 00:20:31,480
Well, we can't all teach. Some of us have to learn

158
00:20:31,480 --> 00:20:33,280
without teaching.

159
00:20:35,240 --> 00:20:35,720
No.

160
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:38,480
Really, we're talking about

161
00:20:38,480 --> 00:20:41,660
a certain degree of bookishness

162
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:43,560
which of course

163
00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:45,580
our business community or any business

164
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:47,620
community has never had to this day

165
00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:49,320
I would assume that if you're interviewing

166
00:20:49,320 --> 00:20:51,520
a young executive for a job

167
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:52,960
and he comes walking in carrying

168
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:55,500
the woman's room which she's in the process

169
00:20:55,500 --> 00:20:57,540
of reading you might think he's not quite the right

170
00:20:57,540 --> 00:20:58,840
man for the job I mean

171
00:20:58,840 --> 00:21:01,460
you don't want young executives to read novels

172
00:21:01,460 --> 00:21:03,180
he comes in reading the latest

173
00:21:03,180 --> 00:21:05,280
tome on the American economy

174
00:21:05,280 --> 00:21:06,380
he might take it more seriously

175
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:08,560
This goes back, it's not American,

176
00:21:08,820 --> 00:21:10,340
goes back 200 years.

177
00:21:10,980 --> 00:21:13,600
Novel reading was always a woman's job,

178
00:21:14,240 --> 00:21:15,700
or it still is, as a matter of fact.

179
00:21:15,800 --> 00:21:17,560
Most novels in this country are still bought by women.

180
00:21:17,680 --> 00:21:19,460
Most books in this country are still bought by women.

181
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:21,880
If you're the publishing business, as I was for many years,

182
00:21:21,920 --> 00:21:22,620
you'd discover that.

183
00:21:24,740 --> 00:21:26,460
Businessmen read business magazines.

184
00:21:26,660 --> 00:21:27,440
They read memoranda.

185
00:21:27,940 --> 00:21:29,620
They don't even read many business magazines.

186
00:21:30,080 --> 00:21:33,860
Half of the businessmen who read the Wall Street Journal

187
00:21:33,860 --> 00:21:35,220
don't even read the editorial page,

188
00:21:35,280 --> 00:21:36,880
which I take personal offense at.

189
00:21:37,100 --> 00:21:46,940
But as I say, the habit of reading reflectively atrophies if it's not used.

190
00:21:46,940 --> 00:21:53,300
And since business does sweep you up into decision-making all the time under great stress,

191
00:21:54,320 --> 00:21:59,880
the intellectual muscles for reflective reading tend to atrophy.

192
00:22:00,020 --> 00:22:04,160
And I don't know what quite to do about it, but I do think it's a problem.

193
00:22:04,160 --> 00:22:08,820
And if anyone can figure out how to cope with that problem, I personally would be very grateful.

194
00:22:14,460 --> 00:22:19,960
I don't think that you necessarily need to keep up with the outside world.

195
00:22:20,420 --> 00:22:24,180
I think you need to keep up with your own inside world much more.

196
00:22:25,060 --> 00:22:36,900
And my urgent advice to anybody is not to have a reading list enough to learn economics and not even to teach,

197
00:22:36,900 --> 00:22:45,500
but to have one real, genuine, major, non-job-related interest.

198
00:22:46,020 --> 00:22:49,060
And that may be playing the cello in a spring quartet.

199
00:22:50,250 --> 00:22:51,230
That is important.

200
00:22:51,270 --> 00:22:52,230
Not golf, surely.

201
00:22:52,510 --> 00:22:52,770
What?

202
00:22:52,930 --> 00:22:53,610
Not golf.

203
00:22:54,830 --> 00:22:56,990
If you are good enough, why not?

204
00:22:58,830 --> 00:23:02,350
Something in which you are more than just a dilettante,

205
00:23:02,770 --> 00:23:05,630
in which you take it seriously enough to work it.

206
00:23:05,630 --> 00:23:12,060
because the important thing, believe me, the human race starts with you and not

207
00:23:12,060 --> 00:23:18,120
with your subordinates, and it starts with you and not with Mr. Carter. So the

208
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:23,700
most important thing is to make sure that you don't atrophy and grow. But that

209
00:23:23,700 --> 00:23:31,840
means you and not I and not he and not she. What is it that you need so that you

210
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:40,640
stay alive and keep on growing and that you that the aging process remains

211
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:46,240
confined to the legs and stops short at the neck
